summary: A panel type is a directory with an index.html and a main.js exporting mount(root, metafolder); the shell mounts it in a shadow root, in its own page, and hands it the API.

A panel type is a directory of `~/.config/metafolder/gui/panel-types/`: its name is
the type's name, and it is listed as soon as it holds an `index.html`
([[Panel types]]). Two files make a panel:

```
panel-types/my-panel/
  index.html    the markup and the style
  main.js       export function mount(root, metafolder)
```

Copy the [[hello panel]] to start: it is the smallest panel that reads a workspace
variable, follows it, and registers a command. The built-in panels are in the same
directory, plain HTML and JavaScript with no build step, to be read as examples —
their sources are in `crates/gui/default-config/panel-types/`.

!! What the shell does with the two files

For each panel it creates, the shell (`PanelHost.svelte`):

# fetches `index.html` from the GUI server (`GET /panel/<type>/index.html`), which serves the directory's files as they are;
# creates a host element with a ''shadow root'', and moves the children of the page's `<body>` into a `<div class="mf-panel-body">` inside it. `<script>` elements are dropped; the `<style>` elements become one style sheet;
# gives the shadow root four style sheets, in this order: the shell's base sheet, the user's `style.css`, the panel's own, the help cursor's. At equal weight the panel's rule therefore wins over the user's ([[Style and theming]]);
# imports `main.js` and calls `mount(root, metafolder)`: `root` is the shadow root, `metafolder` is [[The metafolder API]].

`mount` may be async, and may return a function: the shell calls it when the panel
is destroyed, to remove what the panel attached outside its root — a listener on
`document`, a subscription to the change feed. An error thrown by `mount`, or a file
that cannot be loaded, is caught: the panel shows `panel "<type>" failed to load`
with the error, and the rest of the GUI goes on.

```
import { byId, el } from '/__ui.js';

/** @param {ShadowRoot} root @param {MetafolderApi} metafolder */
export function mount(root, metafolder) {
  const list = byId(root, 'entries');
  const refresh = async () => {
    const repo = await metafolder.workspace.get('active_repo');
    if (typeof repo !== 'string') return;
    const { records } = await metafolder.daemon.query(repo, { query: { type: 'is_present', field: 'rating' }, select: '*', limit: 50 });
    list.replaceChildren(...records.map((record) => el('li', { 'data-mf-uuid': record.uuid }, record.uuid)));
  };
  metafolder.workspace.onChange('active_repo', () => metafolder.whenVisible(refresh));
  metafolder.whenVisible(refresh);
  return metafolder.changes.subscribe(() => metafolder.whenVisible(refresh));
}
```

!! One page, no isolation

Panels run ''in the shell's own page'', not in iframes: a panel is ordinary
JavaScript of that page, and `metafolder` is a plain object whose methods call the
Rust side directly. The shadow root isolates the panel's CSS and nothing else.
Three consequences:

* `document` is the shell. A panel looks its elements up in `root` — `root.getElementById(…)`, or `byId(root, …)` from `/__ui.js`, which throws on a missing id (semgrep rule `mf-panel-queries-document`).
* Key events bubble from the panel to the shell's single key matcher: a panel does not handle keys, it registers commands and suggests keys for them ([[Keybindings]]).
* A panel is ''trusted code'': it can reach the file system and run shell commands through the API, and it can break state it shares with the others ([[GUI design decisions]]).

!! One instance per workspace and type

The shell keeps one instance of a panel type ''per workspace'', created the first
time it is needed and destroyed with the workspace. It is never moved in the
document, and it stays alive, hidden, while another type is shown in its slot: that
is what keeps a panel's state for the session. Hence the rule that one workspace
cannot show the same type in both slots ([[Panel slots and layout]]).

Every instance of a type registers the ''same command names''. The registry holds
one entry per name; the handler and the declared arguments are kept per instance,
and a command runs on the instance of the focused workspace, which is created
first if that workspace never showed the panel. A command registered with
`reveal: true` also puts its panel on screen first: in the other slot when that
slot shows the same workspace, in the focused one otherwise.

Whatever the shell keeps about panels must be keyed by workspace as well as by
name: a table keyed by command name alone belongs to whichever instance registered
last, usually another workspace's ([[Panel pitfalls]]).

!! Built is not shown

One second after startup the shell creates every installed panel type once, hidden,
in the focused workspace, so that every panel's commands exist from the start.
`mount` must therefore be cheap: register commands, suggest keys, attach listeners —
and put anything that reads the daemon or the disk behind `metafolder.whenVisible(fn)`,
which runs `fn` at once when the panel is on screen and at its next display
otherwise. The same function asked for several times while hidden runs once.

!! Reading and writing

* Data comes from the daemon at each read, and nothing on the GUI side keeps it: a panel keeps what it displays and reads it again when the change feed says so ([[Daemon data in panels]]).
* What panels share goes through [[workspace variables|Workspace variables]] — the active repository, the selection — and a panel's own state is a variable named `<panel>:<key>`.
* Everything a panel lets the user do is a command, so that it can be typed, bound to a key and run by a script ([[Commands]], [[Command naming]]).

!! Trying it

Nothing is reloaded while the GUI runs: a module is loaded once per page, and a
panel lives until its workspace is closed. Restart the GUI after an edit
([[Reloading the configuration]]). Three more things when working on the built-in
panels in a checkout:

* they are served from the configuration repository, not from the source tree: run `metafolder-sync-config` after editing `crates/gui/default-config/` ([[Updating the configuration]]);
* the `/__*.js` helpers (`panel-shim/`) are compiled into the GUI binary, and the shell is bundled into it: editing either needs `npm --prefix crates/gui/frontend run build` and `cargo build -p metafolder-gui`;
* `npm --prefix crates/gui/frontend run typecheck` checks the panels' JavaScript against the API's types (`types/metafolder-api.d.ts`), and `npm test` mounts them in jsdom — without their `<style>`, so a CSS mistake is only seen in the real window ([[devtools:open]] opens the inspector).

